<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Region, Nigeria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Northern Nigeria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern Region was the largest of the regions of colonial and independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, covering the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and Borno, with its capital at Kaduna. Formed from the Northern Provinces, it became self-governing in 1959 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello">Ahmadu Bello</a>. The northern part of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a> was administered with it, and in 1967 it was divided into six states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria.jpg" alt="Northern Region, Nigeria" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Northern_Nigeria_Protectorate_(1900%E2%80%931914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>British involvement in the north began with the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a>, whose territory spread north from the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Benue rivers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a>. Frederick Lugard proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> at Ida, on the basis of the Treaty of Berlin of 1885. War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> followed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a> were conquered first, the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> was taken in February 1903, and Sokoto soon gave in. Lugard governed through local rulers by a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, and in 1914 merged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> with Southern Nigeria. The Richards constitution of 1945 gave the north wide autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The region's government followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster system</a>. A premier led the government, while a governor acted as viceroy, commanded the constabulary and appointed the House of Chiefs, an upper house of unelected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of the Native Authority Councils; the elected House of Assembly sat beside it. The region was divided into thirteen provinces, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, the largest in population and economy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, where the capital Kaduna stood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">Groundnuts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> from Kano were its main source of revenue, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> was mined in Plateau Province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> and Kanuri, chiefly Muslims, dominated the north-west and east, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, Tiv, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> and Idoma were strongest in the north-central areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello">Ahmadu Bello</a>, its first premier, the Northern Peoples Congress dominated the regional parliament, and the Northern Elements Progressive Union formed the main opposition. After British rule ended in 1960, a governor continued to be appointed as representative of the federal administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. In 1962 the British Northern Cameroons voted to become a province of the region. In 1967 the Federal Military Government of General Yakubu Gowon broke up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s four regions into twelve states, and the north became the North-Eastern, North-Western, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Kaduna, Kwara and Benue-Plateau states, each with its own governor. The name Northern Nigeria is still used loosely for the same area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern Region, Nigeria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Nigerian independence in 1960 the emirate became part of the <b>Northern Region</b> and later of Borno State.</blockquote><a href="/state/borno-emirate">Borno Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Both parts were administered as part of Colonial Nigeria, the north with the <b>Northern Region</b> and the south with the Eastern Region, an arrangement many southern Cameroonians resented.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1961 plebiscite the Northern Cameroons voted to join Nigeria, and Dikwa became part of <b>Nigeria's Northern Region</b>, joining its Kanuri kin in the Borno Emirate.</blockquote><a href="/state/dikwa-emirate">Dikwa Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
